(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Above what sound level does long-term chronic exposure cause irreversible cochlear damage, and what is the cellular change?","choices":["Above 120 decibels, causing tympanic membrane rupture","Above 60 decibels, causing endolymphatic hydrops","Above 85 decibels, by stereocilia degeneration and apoptosis","Above 85 decibels, causing reversible stereocilia fatigue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both words matter: degeneration of the stereocilia and programmed death of the hair cells. Neither regenerates, which is why the loss is permanent. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which medication class is a cataract risk?","choices":["Chronic corticosteroids","Chronic beta-blockers","Chronic antihistamines","Chronic statins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — chronic corticosteroid use. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What are the two connective tissue layers of the dermis?","choices":["Papillary, the thin superficial layer, and reticular, the dense deeper layer","Basale and spinosum","Corneum and lucidum","The papillary and the subcutaneous, being the superficial and the deepest layers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The dermis is also where the blood vessels and mechanoreceptors sit. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What produces leukonychia, and what else can cause it?","choices":["Overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed","Increased capillary density with release of vascular endothelial growth factor into the nail bed tissue itself","Temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed, usually harmless nail bed damage but also seen with heavy metal poisoning","A halt of keratin production, associated with illness and malnourishment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — white streaks or spots, also called Mee's lines. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which structural epidermal changes occur in psoriasis?","choices":["Epidermal hyperplasia, loss of the stratum granulosum, and failure of epidermal cells to secrete lipids producing xeroderma","Thinning of the epidermis with slowed keratinocyte division","Separation of the epidermis from the dermis with fluid accumulating between the two layers of the skin itself","Loss of melanocytes from the basal layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Losing the granular layer is what leaves the characteristic scale. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Where must light be focused for an image to form?","choices":["On the optic disc, where the nerve exits","On the lens itself, which then transmits the signal","On the retina, after refraction through the cornea and lens","On the choroid, after refraction through the cornea alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is how the image forms. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why does anticoagulation favour POSTERIOR rather than anterior epistaxis?","choices":["The posterior mucosa is thinner","The anterior septum has no blood supply","Anticoagulants are concentrated posteriorly","Posterior bleeding is driven by systemic vascular and clotting factors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anterior causes are local insults to the mucosa; posterior causes act on the vessels and on clotting. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Depolarisation of the cochlear hair cell comes from an ion influx. Through what, and out of which fluid?","choices":["Influx from the perilymph across the basilar membrane","Release from intracellular stores after glutamate binding","Efflux into the perilymph through voltage-gated channels","Influx from the endolymph through the tip-link channels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The endolymph is the reservoir, and opening the tip-link channels lets ions rush in. Depolarisation then opens voltage-gated channels and glutamate is released onto cranial nerve VIII. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What distinguishes dry from wet gangrene?","choices":["Dry is acute; wet is chronic","Dry involves fibrinous exudate; wet involves serous exudate","Dry is coagulative necrosis; wet is liquefactive necrosis arising from infection","Dry gangrene occurs on mucosal surfaces; wet gangrene occurs on serosal surfaces"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gangrenous inflammation is a severe form of necrosis with or without superimposed bacterial infection, dry being coagulative and wet liquefactive from infection. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Granulocytes are characterised under light microscopy by what, and what do those structures primarily do?","choices":["A kidney-shaped nucleus that permits the cell to transmigrate between two adjacent endothelial cells nearby","Azurophilic granules that function purely as antigen-presenting structures","Surface receptors that bind the Fc portion of immunoglobulin G","Differently staining cytoplasmic granules that are membrane-bound enzymes acting in the digestion of phagocytized particles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The granules are membrane-bound enzymes whose primary action is digesting what has been phagocytized. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What do promoters do?","choices":["They cause the permanent deoxyribonucleic acid damage that starts the process","They repair the deoxyribonucleic acid damage caused by initiators","They cause tumour cells to detach and enter the circulation","They drive proliferation of cells already damaged by an initiator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sustained or enhanced proliferation raises the risk of successive mutations leading to cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does the degree of differentiation of a neoplasm measure?","choices":["How rapidly the tumour mass is enlarging over time","How many of its cells are in the proliferative pool at any moment","How closely it resembles comparable normal cells in appearance and function","How far it has spread from the primary site within the patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — this is the basis of histological grading. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A sample from an oesophageal carcinoma shows COLUMNAR cells. What does that imply?","choices":["It is a primary oesophageal cancer","It is a metastasis from a gastrointestinal primary","It is a benign adenoma rather than a carcinoma","It is secondary to reflux disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — squamous cells would indicate a primary cancer, since the lower oesophagus is normally squamous. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 8:47"},{"q":"Why are the outer, easily visible structures grouped first?","choices":["They contain the highest density of nerve endings","They are the last to be affected in systemic disease","They are more susceptible to infection, trauma and environmental change","They are the only structures visible without an ophthalmoscope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — that is what the first group has in common. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A temporal bone fracture causes conductive loss by which mechanism?","choices":["Obstruction of the external canal","Discontinuity of the ossicular chain","Stiffness of the ossicular chain","Mass loading of the tympanic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The chain is physically disrupted so the wave cannot be conducted across it. Ossicular necrosis produces the same end result by erosion rather than trauma. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the three linked vascular mechanisms?","choices":["Exudation, then vasodilation, then endothelial contraction","Vasodilation of arterioles, then endothelial contraction increasing capillary permeability, then exudation","Endothelial contraction, then vasodilation of the arterioles, then leukocyte transmigration into the tissue","Endothelial contraction, then exudation, then vasodilation of arterioles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vasodilation raises blood flow, endothelial contraction opens the capillaries, and fluid and blood proteins then move into the interstitial spaces. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What distinguishes atrophy from hypoplasia?","choices":["Atrophy is shrinkage of a tissue or organ that had already formed and matured normally","Atrophy is partial development of an organ that never reached mature size","Atrophy is enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge","Atrophy is disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the timing is what separates them: hypoplasia is a developmental failure. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What do regulatory (suppressor) T cells do?","choices":["Returning the immune system to normal operation after infection, and preventing autoimmunity","Producing antibody directed against antigens the host has encountered previously","Presenting phagocytosed material to naive T cells","Killing tumour cells directly through granule release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both halves of that role are given, which is why loss of this subset is conceptually linked to autoimmune disease. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which systemic diseases are strongly linked to scleritis?","choices":["Sarcoidosis and choroidal melanoma","Rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis and systemic vasculitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus, psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis","Diabetes mellitus and hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those three. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which causes are given for bullae?","choices":["Androgen stimulation of sebaceous glands","Mechanical factors, infections, toxins, chemical exposure and autoimmune reactions","Increased melanin production and cumulative ultraviolet light exposure alone","Capillary rupture and thrombocytopenia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with bullous impetigo as the bacterial example and bullous pemphigoid as the autoimmune one. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What do the semicircular canals detect, and how?","choices":["Linear acceleration and gravitational position, via the otoliths","Rotational acceleration, by endolymph bending the cupula","Sound frequency, via the basilar membrane","Middle ear pressure, via the Eustachian tube"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three loops in the sagittal, coronal and transverse planes. The fluid lags behind the head, the gelatinous cupula bends, and the hair cell stereocilia inside it are stimulated. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which four cell types make up the epidermis, and which predominates?","choices":["Keratinocytes, melanocytes, Merkel cells and Langerhans cells, with keratinocytes about 90 percent","Fibroblasts, macrophages, adipocytes and mast cells, with fibroblasts predominating","Melanocytes, Merkel cells, fibroblasts and histiocytes, with melanocytes predominating","Keratinocytes and melanocytes only, in roughly equal proportion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because keratinocytes dominate so heavily, most epidermal pathology is fundamentally a keratinocyte problem. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are the purposes of cancer staging?","choices":["To measure how closely the tumour resembles the normal tissue it came from","To identify which gene alterations caused the tumour","To determine whether the tumour is benign or malignant","Extent of spread, prognosis, and guiding management"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three. Staging answers a different question from histological grading. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What does a monocyte become after leaving the bloodstream, and what does it then do?","choices":["A dendritic cell that migrates to the thymus","A tissue macrophage that removes dead cell debris and attacks microorganisms","A plasma cell that secretes antibody directly into the interstitium","A fibroblast that lays down scar tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Monocytes eventually leave the bloodstream to become tissue macrophages with exactly these roles. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What are the risk factors for rhegmatogenous detachment?","choices":["Chronic corticosteroid use, Down syndrome and ultraviolet light exposure","Posterior vitreous detachment, age, severe myopia, trauma and lattice degeneration","Diabetes, prior vitrectomy and retinal trauma surgery","Sarcoidosis, malignant hypertension and melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those five. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the correct plural of carcinoma?","choices":["Carcinomata","Carcinomas","Carcinomae","Carcinomi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — he called adding an s a misnomer, while allowing that most people do it. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 34:36"},{"q":"What does the lipid layer of the tear film do?","choices":["It provides the bulk volume of the tears","It anchors the tear film to the corneal epithelium","It carries immunoglobulin to the ocular surface","It prevents evaporation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why its loss causes dry eye. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which causes are listed as CENTRAL vertigo?","choices":["Presbycusis and noise trauma","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, Meniere disease and labyrinthitis","Brainstem stroke, multiple sclerosis and cerebellar tumour","Otosclerosis and cerumen impaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All involve the brainstem or cerebellum, which is why the nystagmus can be vertical and is not suppressed by fixation, and why other neurological signs appear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which organisms cause 80 to 90 per cent of otitis externa, and what predisposes to the fungal form?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae; fungal disease follows swimming","Moraxella catarrhalis alone; fungal disease follows trauma","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus; fungal after antibiotics","Respiratory syncytial virus and rhinovirus; fungal disease follows steroid use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The bacterial pair dominate, and Aspergillus niger or Candida albicans take over when the bacterial flora has been suppressed or the canal stays wet. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is melanoma risk inherited?","choices":["As an autosomal recessive trait with complete penetrance","As an X-linked trait","It is entirely environmental with no inherited component","As an autosomal dominant trait with variable penetrance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, though ultraviolet light and oxidative stress remain the primary driving factor. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is a hypopyon?","choices":["Yellow debris beneath the retinal pigment epithelium","Pus in the anterior chamber","Blood in the anterior chamber","Fluid beneath the neurosensory retina"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pus in the anterior chamber. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do the neutrophil's nucleus and cytoplasm appear?","choices":["Kidney-shaped nucleus with abundant cytoplasm that is usually agranulated when it is viewed on light microscopy","Multilobed nucleus that may appear like multiple nuclei, with cytoplasm looking transparent because of fine pale lilac granules","Bi- or tri-lobed nucleus with a number of coarse granules","Bi-lobed nucleus with pink-orange granules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both the multilobed nucleus and the fine pale lilac granules are given. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which pair of conditions are examples of autoimmunity causing chronic inflammation?","choices":["Rheumatoid arthritis and lupus","Atherosclerosis and silicosis","Asthma and hay fever","Trauma and bee sting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are named as autoimmune causes of chronic inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What produces Terry's nails, and what conditions is it associated with?","choices":["Increased capillary density together with vascular endothelial growth factor release into the tissue locally over time","Impaired keratin synthesis with deficiency anaemia","Temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed","Overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed, associated with aging, liver disease, congestive heart failure or diabetes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the nail is opaque except for a dark band at the tip. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"In conductive hearing loss, which way does Weber lateralise and what does Rinne show?","choices":["Weber to the affected ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone","Weber is equal; Rinne shows bone greater than air bilaterally","Weber to the AFFECTED ear; Rinne shows bone conduction greater than air","Weber to the UNAFFECTED ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The blocked ear is not receiving competing air-borne sound, so bone-conducted sound is heard more loudly in it — and with the conducting chain impaired, bone beats air on Rinne. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Suppurative (purulent) inflammation contains which combination of material?","choices":["Remnants of white blood cells, protein and tissue debris — that is, pus","Fibrinogen forming a thick meshwork over the serosal surface","Low-protein plasma with minimal cellular content","A superficial necrotic layer forming a membrane over affected mucosa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is the suppurative or purulent pattern. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does panophthalmitis differ from endophthalmitis?","choices":["It involves only the vitreous","It is limited to the anterior segment and spares the vitreous","It spares the sclera","It involves all layers of the globe and may extend into orbital tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is exactly the distinction. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is sebum primarily composed of?","choices":["Collagen and elastin","Triglycerides, wax esters and squalene","Water, sodium and chloride electrolytes","Keratin and keratohyalin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, produced by the sebaceous glands associated with hair follicles to lubricate skin and hair. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do the borders of benign and malignant tumours differ?","choices":["Both have well circumscribed borders, and they are distinguished only by growth rate","Both have ragged borders, and they are distinguished only by whether they metastasize","Benign is well circumscribed and compresses; malignant is ragged and invades","Benign has a ragged border and infiltrates surrounding tissue; malignant is well circumscribed and compresses it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the benign tumour pushes surrounding tissue aside, the malignant one infiltrates and goes through it. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which inherited tumour suppressor alteration is associated with malignant melanoma?","choices":["p16, also called INK4a","Retinoblastoma protein","Adenomatous polyposis coli","BRCA-1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — one of five hereditary examples. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary from a secondary skin lesion?","choices":["A primary lesion is the direct result of the underlying disease and retains its original unmodified appearance; a secondary lesion has been modified over time","A primary lesion is palpable and a secondary lesion is flat","A primary lesion is benign and a secondary lesion is malignant","A primary lesion is the one that appears first in time, and a secondary lesion is any lesion that appears later at a different site elsewhere on the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and a secondary lesion may or may not still resemble the lesion it came from. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What kind of immune reaction is allergic rhinitis, and what does it look like?","choices":["Immune complex deposition, type 3 hypersensitivity","T cell mediated, type 4 delayed hypersensitivity","IgE-mediated type 1 hypersensitivity, with CLEAR rhinorrhoea","IgG-mediated type 2 hypersensitivity with purulent rhinorrhoea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Type 1, immediate, IgE-driven. The CLEAR discharge is what separates it at the bedside from bacterial sinusitis, which is purulent. Allergic shiners complete the picture. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What colour do eosinophil granules characteristically assume, and what is the nuclear shape?","choices":["No visible granules, kidney-shaped nucleus","Pale lilac granules, multilobed nucleus","Coarse dark granules, bi- or tri-lobed nucleus","Pink-orange granules, bi-lobed nucleus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cytoplasm is full of granules that assume a characteristic pink-orange colour, with a bi-lobed nucleus. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which layer of the epidermis is the site of cellular division?","choices":["The stratum corneum, the outermost layer","The granular layer, known as the stratum granulosum","The stratum lucidum","The basal layer, or stratum basale, the deepest layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A single layer of rapidly dividing columnar keratinocytes, which also contains melanocytes and Merkel cells. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Vasodilation of the arterioles supplying an injured region produces which immediate effect?","choices":["Increased blood flow to the injured region","Reduced hydrostatic pressure within the capillary bed","Contraction of the capillary endothelium","Movement of blood proteins into the interstitium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vasodilation of the supplying arterioles increases blood flow. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the classic clinical example of metaplasia, and what changes into what?","choices":["Endometrial hyperplasia — glandular becomes squamous under excess oestrogen","Barrett's oesophagus — squamous becomes columnar under chronic acid reflux","Barrett's oesophagus — columnar becomes squamous under chronic acid reflux","Cervical dysplasia — squamous becomes columnar under human papillomavirus infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lower oesophagus is squamous and cannot resist acid, so chronic reflux drives the change. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 7:45"},{"q":"In the five main processes of the inflammatory response, what immediately follows the migration of neutrophils?","choices":["Chemotaxis","Increased permeability","Exudation of plasma proteins","Increased blood flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The order runs increased blood flow, increased permeability, migration of neutrophils, chemotaxis, then leucocyte recruitment and activation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which factors contribute to seborrhoeic dermatitis, and where does it occur?","choices":["Autoimmune T cell activation, affecting the elbows and knees","Ultraviolet damage, affecting sun-exposed skin","Cutaneous microbiome dysbiosis, altered immune response and epidermal barrier dysfunction, affecting regions rich in sebaceous glands — scalp, face and upper trunk","A single fungal pathogen acting alone, affecting mainly the palms of the hands and the soles of the feet rather than the sebaceous areas of the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the cause is incompletely understood. Dandruff is the non-inflammatory form. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How do dermatophytes obtain nutrition, and in which layer do they typically live?","choices":["Spores secrete keratinases and proteases to metabolise and digest keratin in skin, hair and nails, typically within the stratum corneum","They invade epidermal basal cells through microabrasions and drive proliferation of the epidermis above them over time","They release histamine from mast cells producing wheals","They proliferate within the dermis, digesting collagen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — Trichophyton, Microsporum and Epidermophyton are the three most common, and the annular patch with central clearing follows from this. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which organism is the usual bacterial cause of tonsillitis, and which finding suggests abscess?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae; bilateral exudate","Pseudomonas aeruginosa; fever above 39 degrees","Haemophilus influenzae; odynophagia","Group A Streptococcus; asymmetric deviation with trismus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Deviation and trismus indicate the infection has collected rather than staying confined to the tonsil and pharyngeal mucosa. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does eradicating Helicobacter pylori do to cancer risk?","choices":["It reverses established gastric adenocarcinoma","It reduces gastric cancer risk, and some early MALT lymphomas regress","It has no effect on cancer risk once colonization has occurred","It increases the risk, because the inflammatory response is protective"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — evidence that the chronic inflammation itself is the carcinogenic driver. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What do Nx, N0 and N1 to N2 or higher each mean?","choices":["Nx, nodes not yet sampled; N0, one node involved; N1 upwards, two or more nodes","Nx, nodes removed surgically; N0, nodes present but normal in size; N1 upwards, enlarged nodes","Cannot be assessed; none; increasing number and range involved","Nx, no regional nodal metastasis; N0, nodes cannot be assessed; N1 upwards, distant metastasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — x always means cannot be assessed, 0 always means none found, and the numbers rise with the number and range of nodes involved. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does aqueous humour nourish?","choices":["The extraocular muscles","The avascular lens and cornea","The retina and the choroid","The optic nerve head"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the avascular lens and cornea. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which feature of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo separates it most cleanly from the other peripheral causes?","choices":["The vertigo lasts several hours","It follows a viral upper respiratory infection","There is NO hearing loss","It produces aural fullness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meniere disease and labyrinthitis both take hearing with them; this does not, because the otoconia are a vestibular problem that never touches the cochlea. Attacks also last under a minute and follow head position. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Through which two routes can the inflammatory process heal an injury or implant site?","choices":["Vasoconstriction of the supplying arterioles, or lymphatic drainage of the exudate","Generation of new tissue by native parenchymal cells, or formation of fibroblastic scar tissue","Phagocytosis by neutrophils alone, or opsonisation by immunoglobulin G","Hemoconcentration followed by stasis, or leukocyte transmigration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those are the two outcomes of the sequence that heals the site. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are drusen composed of?","choices":["Calcium and phosphate crystals","Cholesterol esters, as in xanthelasma","Lipofuscin and apolipoproteins","Fibrin and platelet aggregates"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lipofuscin and apolipoproteins. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Diabetic retinopathy is the leading cause of new-onset blindness in which group?","choices":["Adults over fifty worldwide","United States adults aged twenty to seventy-four","United States adults aged over seventy-five years","Children under sixteen worldwide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the age band. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of presbyopia?","choices":["The axial globe shortens with age","The cornea flattens progressively with age, reducing its refractive contribution","The retinal pigment epithelium thins with age","The lens loses elasticity and the ciliary muscle loses accommodation, through sclerosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lens loses elasticity and the ciliary muscle weakens. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which three concepts does the hepatitis B slide emphasise?","choices":["Chronic inflammation, regenerative hyperplasia, genomic instability","Initiation, promotion, and progression","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, and abnormal differentiation","Self-renewal, immortality, and differentiation capacity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same triad also explains hepatitis C and Helicobacter pylori. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which single event in otosclerosis produces the hearing loss?","choices":["Erosion of the incus by the remodelled bone","Hair cell loss at the cochlear base","Accumulation of endolymph in the scala media","Ankylosis of the stapes footplate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once the footplate is fixed it cannot transfer mechanical vibration into the cochlear fluid, and conduction fails progressively. Fixation is the whole lesion. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
